WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:03.210
- Thanks to Audible for
keeping Legal Eagle in the air.

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.170
Oh my gosh, I have so
much work to do today.

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:06.610
Well, let me read one more article

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:09.470
by the mainstream media fake
news before I get started.

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:12.070
Testimony torpedoes
republican defenses of Trump.

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:14.380
Trump's defenses are almost entirely gone.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:17.730
Trump has no defense
after Sondland testimony.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:20.252
That can't be right, can it?

00:00:20.252 --> 00:00:23.169
(triumphant music)

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:26.840
Hey Legal Eagles, it's time
to think like a lawyer,

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:29.610
because republican and
democratic positions

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:31.290
on this whole impeachment hearing

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:33.430
have been changing over time,

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:35.080
and I think it's worthwhile going over

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:37.350
the republican impeachment defenses

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:39.150
to see if they hold any legal water.

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:42.040
Quick disclaimer, of
course, the facts are fluid

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:43.050
and they are changing.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:45.900
Multiple witnesses are
testifying every week,

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.160
so some of the factual issues here

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:51.140
that I'm going to discuss
I'm sure are going to change.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:53.240
But I wanted to take some time to focus on

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.330
some of the legal issues
that are implicated

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:56.680
by these impeachment hearings.

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:59.980
I'm going to try to do my best
to steel man these arguments.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:02.620
In other words, I'm going to try to give

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:06.130
the republican defenses
in the best light possible

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:08.340
to avoid attacking the strawmen

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.770
and dealing with the best possible version

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.400
of those particular defenses.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.260
Some of these defenses
are better than others.

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:16.610
It's a bit of a moving target though,

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:17.790
because different republicans have

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:19.380
focused on different defenses.

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:21.643
There isn't exactly a
unified front on this.

00:01:21.643 --> 00:01:24.940
With that being said, let's
dig in to the main defenses

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:28.220
that republicans are using
in this impeachment inquiry.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:31.060
So the first impeachment
defense probably boils down

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:33.320
to no quid pro quo, or on other words,

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:34.910
the call was perfect.

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:37.510
- There's a rumor out there,
the first conversation.

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:41.770
It was beautiful, it was
just a perfect conversation.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:44.820
- This is largely the preferred
defense of President Trump

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.760
in that he tends to tweet
this out with some frequency.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.810
This also appeared to
be the early favorite

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:52.630
defense of the republicans
that has largely evaporated.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:53.650
You still see it a little bit,

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:56.390
but it's not the favored
defense at the time.

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:58.410
The argument is that as a factual matter,

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:00.340
there was no quid pro quo

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:02.740
between the United States and Ukraine.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:05.080
- I didn't do it, there
was no quid pro quo.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:07.680
Senators and all of these other people

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.790
have actually done what
they're accusing me of doing,

00:02:10.790 --> 00:02:11.670
which I didn't do.

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:14.570
- As the president often
tweets out and says in public,

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:15.500
read the transcript.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:17.930
Whether you believe that
the transcript is sufficient

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:20.040
to show a quid pro quo evidencing

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:21.240
a solicitation of a bribe,

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:22.850
or whether you believe that the transcript

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:25.310
demonstrates that the
call was in fact perfect,

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:29.160
as the president says is a
factual matter for you to decide.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:30.980
A variation of this argument was used

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:33.130
in the questioning of
Lieutenant Colonel Vindman

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:35.090
by Representative
Ratcliffe, who pointed out

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:37.560
that no witness in the depositions

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.480
as part of the impeachment inquiry

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.840
had ever used the word bribery.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:45.030
- In an impeachment inquiry
that the Speaker of the House

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:48.570
says is all about
bribery, where bribery is

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:52.900
the impeachable offense, no
witness has used the word

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:57.010
bribery to describe President
Trump's conduct, none of them.

00:02:57.010 --> 00:03:01.160
- Instead, that witnesses had
used the phrase quid pro quo.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:03.400
Bribery is the ultimate conclusion.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.680
In other words, it is a legal conclusion

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:08.340
that the democrats are
attempting to prove,

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:11.450
if the analogy to being
a prosecutor holds.

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:14.630
And it would be improper
to ask the fact witnesses

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:16.270
about an ultimate legal conclusion,

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:18.070
that's not what fact witnesses are for.

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:19.570
And while reasonable minds can differ

00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:21.550
about the conclusions that one draws

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:23.530
from the facts that have
been elicited thus far,

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:26.100
and I leave it to you
as to where you think

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.070
the facts are going in
this particular case,

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:30.340
it does seem like most
republicans are pivoting away

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:33.470
from the argument that there
is no quid pro quo at all,

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:34.960
because to believe that argument,

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:36.770
you would have to believe
that most of the witnesses

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:38.640
that have testified so far are lying,

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.710
including Yovanovitch, Sondland,
Holmes, Williams, Taylor,

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:47.210
Volker, Kent, Hill, Vindman,
and John friggin' Bolton,

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:49.250
they're all liars in this particular case,

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:51.330
and that's a hard argument to make,

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:52.740
which is why it seems to appear that

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:54.980
most republicans have moved
on from no quid pro quo

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:58.730
to no illegal or impeachable quid pro quo.

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:01.090
In fact, even chief of staff Mick Mulvaney

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:03.860
said in the last press
conference that he gave

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:05.550
that there was in fact a quid pro quo

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:07.360
and that quid pro quos
happen all the time.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:09.950
- We do that all the
time with foreign policy.

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:11.890
- And in fact that this
particular quid pro quo

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:15.640
was conditioned partially on
investigation into the Bidens.

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:17.610
Gordon Sondland said in his testimony

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:20.320
that there was in fact
an explicit quid pro quo.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:21.983
He straight out said it.

00:04:21.983 --> 00:04:24.680
- What there a quid pro quo?

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:27.970
As I testified previously with regard to

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:31.280
the requested White House call
and the White House meeting,

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:32.910
the answer is yes.

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:34.980
- Now House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:37.580
takes a slightly more nuanced
path when he argued that

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:39.660
the Ukrainians got
everything that they wanted,

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.420
so there was no quid pro quo.

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:43.040
That's not exactly the
same thing as arguing

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:44.360
that there was no quid pro quo

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:48.364
except to say that there
was no ultimate transfer

00:04:48.364 --> 00:04:51.270
of a thing of value, which
is a slightly different

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:53.090
argument that we'll get
to in just a second.

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:55.550
Ben Shapiro points out an
interesting nuance on Twitter

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:57.797
when he says "the question
for Sondland today

00:04:57.797 --> 00:04:59.267
"isn't whether Trump withheld aid

00:04:59.267 --> 00:05:01.007
"in exchange for investigations.

00:05:01.007 --> 00:05:02.057
"We already knew that.

00:05:02.057 --> 00:05:03.907
"The question is whether Trump's intent

00:05:03.907 --> 00:05:06.746
"was to get Biden in anticipation of 2020

00:05:06.746 --> 00:05:09.227
"or to investigate 2016 activities

00:05:09.227 --> 00:05:11.987
"out of concern for
corruption, even if the latter

00:05:11.987 --> 00:05:13.677
"was based on bad information

00:05:13.677 --> 00:05:16.390
"and conspiracism promoted by Giuliani."

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:19.970
And this is true in a
criminal prosecution sense.

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:21.380
I don't often agree with Ben Shapiro,

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:24.080
but I think this is an
interesting, if nuanced, point.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:28.210
It deals with the mens rea
defense to impeachment,

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:29.980
which we'll talk about in just a second,

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:31.310
but I do want to drop a footnote

00:05:31.310 --> 00:05:32.330
that I want you to think about,

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:34.830
which is that it's not
an either or proposition

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:37.250
that the president's motivation was either

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:40.410
to get dirt on the Bidens
for his own personal gain

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:44.230
or to investigation corruption in Ukraine.

00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:45.610
It's not necessarily the case

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:47.553
that he had only one motivation,

00:05:47.553 --> 00:05:51.125
want the legal implications
of this mixed potential motive

00:05:51.125 --> 00:05:53.110
are very, very interesting.

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:56.700
And that brings me to the next
argument, it's all hearsay.

00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:58.700
The Trump administration
and its supporters

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:00.960
have been fairly consistent
in arguing that the evidence

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:05.247
that's been elicited so far
has been hearsay testimony

00:06:05.247 --> 00:06:08.210
and that it relies on
out-of-court statements.

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:10.870
- It's all hearsay, you
can't get a parking ticket

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:12.780
conviction based on hearsay.

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:15.380
The whistleblower didn't
hear the phone call.

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:18.270
- Now I have done an entire
video on the nature of hearsay

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:20.550
evidence as it regards
these impeachment hearings,

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:24.210
but suffice it to say while
there is a grain of truth

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:26.630
in these arguments, just
because something is hearsay

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:28.210
doesn't mean it's, number one, admissible,

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:30.180
and number two, bad evidence.

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:33.760
And the public shorthand of
thinking of hearsay evidence

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:36.610
as being second and third-hand information

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:40.180
is not necessary coextensive
with the legal definition,

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:43.810
and often hearsay is powerful evidence,

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:46.590
and it depends on the
particular circumstances

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:49.890
whether particular evidence,
particularly hearsay evidence,

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:51.920
is good evidence or bad evidence.

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:55.000
It depends on whether that
evidence is corroborated.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:58.630
It depends on the nature of
the circumstances themselves,

00:06:58.630 --> 00:07:00.880
and it depends on
whether the circumstances

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:03.130
would allow that hearsay
evidence in or not.

00:07:03.130 --> 00:07:04.970
Now there are arguments to be made

00:07:04.970 --> 00:07:07.760
that to the extent the
information is hearsay,

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:10.340
there are all kinds of
exceptions to the hearsay rule

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:12.340
that are used every day in court

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:15.889
to allow that information to
be admitted into evidence.

00:07:15.889 --> 00:07:18.410
Sometimes hearsay evidence
is incredibly strong.

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:20.770
I would argue that things
like business records

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:22.580
in the form of emails or video

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:27.200
or testimony of the accused
who admits to a crime,

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:30.210
all of which are considered
hearsay but are admissible

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:33.360
in court because they are very
strong pieces of evidence.

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:36.370
But admittedly, sometimes
hearsay is particularly weak.

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:38.620
It depends on the nature of the evidence

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:41.420
and the nature of the circumstances
in that particular case.

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:43.250
Now I will point out that
in these proceedings,

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:45.160
there's a bit of what lawyers would call

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:49.230
an unclean hands problem,
in that the people who have

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:52.110
first-hand knowledge of
what the president said

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:54.970
and what the president
did are being prevented

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:57.450
from testifying in these proceedings.

00:07:57.450 --> 00:08:00.240
And I think the democrats
would argue that, for example,

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:03.702
if the mob intimidates a
witness into not testifying,

00:08:03.702 --> 00:08:07.050
that member of the mob
shouldn't be able to then argue

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:10.230
about the lack of witnesses
testifying against them,

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:13.040
and as Neal Katyal has argued on Twitter,

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:15.520
the only reason that we don't
have the first-hand knowledge

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:18.460
witnesses is because Trump
blocked them from testifying.

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:21.670
That itself is impeachable,
and as time goes on,

00:08:21.670 --> 00:08:24.620
perhaps we will get more
testimony from those individuals

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:28.380
who had first-hand knowledge
of the actual instructions

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:31.010
that President Trump may
or may not have given.

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:34.040
And on November 20th, Gordon Sondland,

00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:37.610
who did have some first-hand
interactions with the president

00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:39.840
and first-hand knowledge
of the events described

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:42.830
did testify that apparently
the entire state department

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:46.250
led by Mike Pompeo and
Chief of Staff Mulvaney

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:49.240
did know about the explicit quid pro quo.

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:53.085
- You've testified that Mulvaney was aware

00:08:53.085 --> 00:08:56.790
of this quid pro quo, of this condition

00:08:56.790 --> 00:08:58.460
that the Ukrainians had to meet

00:08:58.460 --> 00:09:01.370
that is announcing this
public investigations

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:04.180
to get the White House
meetings, is that right?

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:07.336
- Yeah, a lot of people were aware of it.

00:09:07.336 --> 00:09:10.070
- Including Mr. Mulvaney?

00:09:10.070 --> 00:09:10.903
- Correct.

00:09:10.903 --> 00:09:12.370
- Which brings me to the next big defense,

00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:15.213
which is that the aid, the
military and financial aid

00:09:15.213 --> 00:09:18.010
that was allegedly
conditioned on investigations

00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:20.960
into the Bidens was in fact released,

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:24.020
or for short, the Sideshow Bob defense.

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:26.900
This is the defense that was
made famous by The Simpsons

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:31.240
in arguing that attempted
crime is not really a crime.

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:34.304
- Convicted of a crime
I didn't even commit.

00:09:34.304 --> 00:09:38.180
(scoffs) Attempted murder,
now honestly what is that?

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:41.539
Do they give a Nobel Price for
attempted chemistry, do they?

00:09:41.539 --> 00:09:42.372
(toilet smashes)

00:09:42.372 --> 00:09:45.290
- I know this may come as
news to many of you out there,

00:09:45.290 --> 00:09:48.910
but attempted crime is in fact a crime,

00:09:48.910 --> 00:09:50.550
and unfortunately for many of you,

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:52.990
next time you are pulled
over by the police,

00:09:52.990 --> 00:09:55.730
you can't try to get out of
it by offering the police

00:09:55.730 --> 00:09:58.920
a bribe and then claim that
it wasn't attempted bribery

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:00.960
because the police officer didn't accept

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:02.030
the bribe that you offered.

00:10:02.030 --> 00:10:05.450
That will get you in a lot
of trouble, #notlegaladvice.

00:10:05.450 --> 00:10:07.710
Now I think most people
intuitively understand

00:10:07.710 --> 00:10:10.880
that attempted crime is
still, in fact, a crime

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:14.230
in and of itself, but this
argument in particular

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:18.470
in the context of bribery
makes absolutely no sense.

00:10:18.470 --> 00:10:21.110
Solicitation of a bribe is a federal crime

00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:25.450
under 18 USC 201
particularly subsection b2.

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:28.435
Federal bribery occurs
when a public official

00:10:28.435 --> 00:10:30.410
seeks a thing of value in exchange for

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:32.650
some official act or duty.

00:10:32.650 --> 00:10:36.010
It doesn't rely on the
person who is the target

00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:38.000
of the solicitation actually giving

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:41.390
the politician in question
the thing of value,

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:42.640
it's all in the ask.

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:44.610
Arguably, there isn't such a thing

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:47.032
as attempted solicitation of bribery

00:10:47.032 --> 00:10:50.550
in the context of someone
who has asked for a bribe.

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:53.330
In that particular case, the
act has been consummated.

00:10:53.330 --> 00:10:56.240
There's no attempt, there is
an actual violation of the law.

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:58.800
So here the argument
is that President Trump

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:01.490
sought an investigation
into a political rival

00:11:01.490 --> 00:11:03.140
in exchange for releasing a hold

00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:05.290
on funds the Congress appropriated,

00:11:05.290 --> 00:11:08.570
punctuated in the Zelensky
transcript readout

00:11:08.570 --> 00:11:10.510
where President Trump talks about the aid

00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:13.800
and then says "I would like
you to do us a favor though."

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:16.310
Along those lines, Nikki
Haley says it didn't succeed,

00:11:16.310 --> 00:11:18.610
so it was absolutely okay.

00:11:18.610 --> 00:11:21.504
- The Ukrainians never
did the investigation,

00:11:21.504 --> 00:11:24.070
and the president released the funds.

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:25.830
I mean, when you look at those,

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:28.010
there's just nothing impeachable there.

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:29.640
- The main argument being here that

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:33.200
because the $400 million in
aid was in fact released,

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:35.410
that is evidence that there was no

00:11:35.410 --> 00:11:37.650
conditional hold on it in the first place,

00:11:37.650 --> 00:11:40.896
therefore no quid pro
quo, and it certainly is

00:11:40.896 --> 00:11:44.040
potential evidence of
that particular argument.

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:46.810
The counterargument there is
that based on the timeline

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:49.426
that the aid was released
only after Politico

00:11:49.426 --> 00:11:54.030
did its famous article on
this particular quid pro quo,

00:11:54.030 --> 00:11:56.380
after the whistleblower
had already come forward,

00:11:56.380 --> 00:11:58.380
and after the House had
started investigating

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:00.490
the whistleblower, that
based on that timing,

00:12:00.490 --> 00:12:04.850
the actual release of the
funds is not as exculpatory

00:12:04.850 --> 00:12:08.050
as the administration may want it to seem.

00:12:08.050 --> 00:12:10.250
And as far as I know,
there doesn't appear to be

00:12:10.250 --> 00:12:12.590
a strong counter-narrative as to why

00:12:12.590 --> 00:12:14.980
the aid was held in the first place.

00:12:14.980 --> 00:12:17.940
Gordon Sondland says he reached
out to the administration

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:20.690
for an answer as to why there was a hold

00:12:20.690 --> 00:12:23.640
and no one, including up
until the present day,

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:25.770
ever provided him with a reason

00:12:25.770 --> 00:12:28.580
for why there was a hold
on these Ukrainian funds,

00:12:28.580 --> 00:12:31.310
which was particularly
important because the aid

00:12:31.310 --> 00:12:33.720
was going to expire at the
end of September if it wasn't

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:36.420
released based on the
congressional budgetary rules.

00:12:36.420 --> 00:12:38.230
The best defense is that it was somehow

00:12:38.230 --> 00:12:40.080
related to anti-corruption measures,

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:42.150
which we'll talk about in just a second.

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:44.180
But that brings me to the next argument,

00:12:44.180 --> 00:12:47.530
which is that the Ukrainians
didn't feel any pressure.

00:12:47.530 --> 00:12:50.870
This is a slight variation on
the aid was released argument.

00:12:50.870 --> 00:12:53.000
Now it remains to be seen to what extent

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:57.080
the Ukrainians knew or thought
that the $400 million in aid

00:12:57.080 --> 00:12:58.700
was being deliberately withheld

00:12:58.700 --> 00:13:01.907
on condition of investigations
into the Bidens.

00:13:01.907 --> 00:13:04.790
There are conflicting
witnesses on both sides,

00:13:04.790 --> 00:13:07.180
but I think it's unanimous
in that everyone knew

00:13:07.180 --> 00:13:08.870
that the aid wasn't delivered

00:13:08.870 --> 00:13:10.897
and that there was some kind of delay

00:13:10.897 --> 00:13:13.218
on the $400 million in aid.

00:13:13.218 --> 00:13:15.790
Now some, including Mick
Mulvaney have argued that

00:13:15.790 --> 00:13:17.540
the president withheld
the aid to ensure that

00:13:17.540 --> 00:13:20.490
it was put to good use,
but it's worth pointing out

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:22.250
that the president doesn't have authority

00:13:22.250 --> 00:13:25.270
to withhold
congressionally-appropriated funds.

00:13:25.270 --> 00:13:28.950
The 1974 Congressional Budget
and Impoundment Control Act

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:32.500
at 31 USC 1512 states that
the president can only

00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:35.020
impound funds under limited circumstances

00:13:35.020 --> 00:13:37.283
and for no more than 45 days,

00:13:37.283 --> 00:13:40.180
because congressional
power is at its zenith

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:42.050
when you're talking about the budget,

00:13:42.050 --> 00:13:43.610
congressional appropriations.

00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:46.650
Congress has the power of the purse,

00:13:46.650 --> 00:13:49.230
and the funds would have
expired if not released

00:13:49.230 --> 00:13:50.950
by the end of September
because of the way that

00:13:50.950 --> 00:13:52.670
the congressional budget works.

00:13:52.670 --> 00:13:56.970
The relevant federal budget was
passed in September of 2018,

00:13:56.970 --> 00:14:00.010
a year prior, and in February of 2019,

00:14:00.010 --> 00:14:02.380
the Trump administration
said it was releasing the aid

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:06.030
to Ukraine and it wasn't
until almost the entire year

00:14:06.030 --> 00:14:08.320
after it was passed that
the Trump administration

00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:10.480
actually released the appropriated funds,

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:12.080
which I probably don't need to tell you

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:15.047
is far longer than the 45 days that

00:14:15.047 --> 00:14:19.290
the Impoundment Control Act
allows the president to delay.

00:14:19.290 --> 00:14:21.660
And as to whether the
Ukrainians actually felt

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:24.150
the pressure or not, it
actually doesn't matter

00:14:24.150 --> 00:14:25.968
for the crime of bribery.

00:14:25.968 --> 00:14:28.460
Elie Mystal makes this
point in a great article

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:30.550
in The Nation which I will link to below.

00:14:30.550 --> 00:14:32.280
He talks about the difference between

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:35.680
the crime of bribery and
the crime of extortion.

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:38.870
Bribery, or at least in this
case solicitation for bribery

00:14:38.870 --> 00:14:40.900
does not require that the recipient feel

00:14:40.900 --> 00:14:44.080
any particular pressure,
whereas the crime of extortion

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:46.270
does require undue pressure being levied

00:14:46.270 --> 00:14:50.410
against the victim and
that forcing them to do

00:14:50.410 --> 00:14:51.990
something as a result of thing.

00:14:51.990 --> 00:14:53.540
Which takes me to the next offense,

00:14:53.540 --> 00:14:58.540
which is too bad to crime,
AKA Quid-Amateur-Pro.

00:14:58.820 --> 00:15:00.780
In the world of attempt, it doesn't matter

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:03.090
if you are stopped beforehand

00:15:03.090 --> 00:15:05.692
or are so inept as to not be able to

00:15:05.692 --> 00:15:09.020
actually consummate the
criminal act contemplated,

00:15:09.020 --> 00:15:12.020
or that the victim is unaware

00:15:12.020 --> 00:15:14.700
that the criminal acts are going on.

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:18.020
What matters are whether you
have the requisite intent

00:15:18.020 --> 00:15:19.670
and whether you take a step

00:15:19.670 --> 00:15:22.170
in furtherance of that particular act.

00:15:22.170 --> 00:15:24.300
So for example, if you
are wearing ski masks

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:27.220
with the intent to rob a
bank, it doesn't matter

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:29.800
if you are arrested before
you get to the bank.

00:15:29.800 --> 00:15:31.440
That's attempted bank robbery.

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:34.320
Or if you go into a bank
and ask the teller for money

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:38.100
at gunpoint and she says "no"
and you don't get any money,

00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:40.340
that's also attempted bank robbery.

00:15:40.340 --> 00:15:41.810
So from a criminal law perspective,

00:15:41.810 --> 00:15:44.600
it doesn't actually matter
if you're not good enough

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:47.390
or competent enough to actually complete

00:15:47.390 --> 00:15:49.460
the crime that you are accused of,

00:15:49.460 --> 00:15:52.250
what matters is that
you attempted to do it

00:15:52.250 --> 00:15:54.410
and that you had the requisite mens rea

00:15:54.410 --> 00:15:56.720
or corrupt intent to be able to do it.

00:15:56.720 --> 00:15:58.260
Of course as we've discussed,

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:00.670
in the world of solicitation of bribery,

00:16:00.670 --> 00:16:03.300
all that's required is
that the ask be made.

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:05.650
It doesn't actually require
that the thing of value

00:16:05.650 --> 00:16:07.840
that was sought actually be transferred.

00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:11.070
There really is no attempt
in this particular context,

00:16:11.070 --> 00:16:13.300
it's the full crime itself.

00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:15.730
So whether the people who
are being accused here

00:16:15.730 --> 00:16:18.690
are competent enough or
not is beside the point,

00:16:18.690 --> 00:16:21.530
at least as it regards
general criminal law.

00:16:21.530 --> 00:16:22.870
Which brings me to the next defense,

00:16:22.870 --> 00:16:25.010
the Ukrainians didn't pay up.

00:16:25.010 --> 00:16:27.630
This is a variation on
the actual crime itself

00:16:27.630 --> 00:16:30.710
was not completed argument,
and this was the focus

00:16:30.710 --> 00:16:33.090
of Representative Jim Jordan
during the questioning

00:16:33.090 --> 00:16:35.540
of Gordon Sondland on November 20th.

00:16:35.540 --> 00:16:37.720
Representative Jordan
focused on the fact that

00:16:37.720 --> 00:16:41.450
allegedly President Trump
had extorted the Ukrainians

00:16:41.450 --> 00:16:45.360
to investigate the Bidens
and to do an investigation

00:16:45.360 --> 00:16:48.890
into the Crowd Strike Ukrainian servers,

00:16:48.890 --> 00:16:52.210
and he focused on the idea
that because the Ukrainians

00:16:52.210 --> 00:16:55.550
didn't pay up for what was
part of the quid pro quo,

00:16:55.550 --> 00:16:58.610
that therefore there
was no underlying crime.

00:16:58.610 --> 00:17:00.930
- You know what a quid pro quo is?

00:17:00.930 --> 00:17:01.763
- I do.

00:17:01.763 --> 00:17:04.183
- This for that, right?

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:08.630
Looks to me like Ukraine got that

00:17:08.630 --> 00:17:10.910
three times and there was no this.

00:17:10.910 --> 00:17:13.663
There was, we didn't do anything,

00:17:14.900 --> 00:17:16.650
or excuse me, they didn't
have to do anything.

00:17:16.650 --> 00:17:18.930
- The argument goes that
effectively no harm, no foul,

00:17:18.930 --> 00:17:21.030
because the Ukrainians
got what they wanted

00:17:21.030 --> 00:17:23.260
and they didn't have to
investigate the Bidens.

00:17:23.260 --> 00:17:25.670
Again, the problem with
this argument is that

00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:27.900
the idea of attempted
solicitation of bribery

00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:29.220
is a little bit incogent,

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:31.870
and the no harm no foul
argument sort of breaks down

00:17:31.870 --> 00:17:34.340
when you compare it to an analogy to

00:17:34.340 --> 00:17:35.910
something that we could all agree would be

00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:37.720
absolutely solicitation for a bribe.

00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:39.350
So imagine if you had a politician

00:17:39.350 --> 00:17:41.550
who is on a city council, for example,

00:17:41.550 --> 00:17:44.887
and says to a local developer

00:17:44.887 --> 00:17:48.157
"I will approve your
project if you give me

00:17:48.157 --> 00:17:51.070
"a million dollars into my bank account."

00:17:51.070 --> 00:17:53.300
Well we can all agree that that is

00:17:53.300 --> 00:17:54.640
solicitation of a bribe.

00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:57.230
It doesn't actually
matter if the developer

00:17:57.230 --> 00:18:00.978
pays the million dollars or eventually

00:18:00.978 --> 00:18:03.290
goes to the newspaper, reveals it,

00:18:03.290 --> 00:18:06.070
and then the development is approved.

00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:08.810
The fact that the politician
asked for a million dollars

00:18:08.810 --> 00:18:11.900
is the improper act
and is the consummation

00:18:11.900 --> 00:18:14.060
of the crime of solicitation of bribery.

00:18:14.060 --> 00:18:16.600
And here, Representative
Jordan is probably correct

00:18:16.600 --> 00:18:19.753
that the Ukrainians didn't
have to do the ultimate things

00:18:19.753 --> 00:18:21.410
that were asked of them.

00:18:21.410 --> 00:18:23.700
The factual evidence appears
to show that the Ukrainians

00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:26.640
were in active talks
with the State Department

00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:28.770
to eventually make an announcement.

00:18:28.770 --> 00:18:30.590
There were negotiations back and forth

00:18:30.590 --> 00:18:33.250
as to what the announcement
was going to say,

00:18:33.250 --> 00:18:35.209
and during the July 25th call,

00:18:35.209 --> 00:18:38.760
President Zelensky says
that he is going to do it.

00:18:38.760 --> 00:18:42.400
Effectively, the damage was
done and the ask was made,

00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:44.890
and on November 20th,
Gordon Sondland testified

00:18:44.890 --> 00:18:46.660
that in exchange for the official act

00:18:46.660 --> 00:18:49.590
of actually releasing
the $400 million in aid,

00:18:49.590 --> 00:18:52.130
the Ukrainians only had to announce

00:18:52.130 --> 00:18:54.240
investigations into the
Bidens, they actually didn't

00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:56.900
have to do the investigations
into the Bidens.

00:18:56.900 --> 00:19:01.310
- I never heard Mr.
Goldman, anyone say that

00:19:01.310 --> 00:19:04.340
the investigations had to
start or had to be completed.

00:19:04.340 --> 00:19:07.776
The only thing I heard from
Mr. Giuliani or otherwise

00:19:07.776 --> 00:19:10.610
was that they had to be
announced in some form,

00:19:10.610 --> 00:19:12.646
and that form kept changing.

00:19:12.646 --> 00:19:13.645
- Announced publicly?

00:19:13.645 --> 00:19:14.478
- Announced publicly.

00:19:14.478 --> 00:19:15.400
- Now it's probably
worth pointing out that

00:19:15.400 --> 00:19:17.820
the aid was released only
after the Trump administration

00:19:17.820 --> 00:19:20.380
allegedly got caught,
and it's possible that

00:19:20.380 --> 00:19:21.890
there are other explanations,

00:19:21.890 --> 00:19:23.660
there could be other evidence here,

00:19:23.660 --> 00:19:26.170
and that the timing here
is only a coincidence.

00:19:26.170 --> 00:19:28.490
But so far, the White House
hasn't really provided

00:19:28.490 --> 00:19:31.480
that evidence or provided
an alternative narrative.

00:19:31.480 --> 00:19:33.930
All right, that takes us
to the no mens rea defense

00:19:33.930 --> 00:19:37.256
and the variation, the
too dumb to crime defense.

00:19:37.256 --> 00:19:40.794
Now, some crimes have a very
specific intent required,

00:19:40.794 --> 00:19:42.930
sometimes called mens rea.

00:19:42.930 --> 00:19:44.840
In other words, a lot
of criminal laws require

00:19:44.840 --> 00:19:47.490
not only do you do the act
that is considered criminal,

00:19:47.490 --> 00:19:50.040
the actus rea, but you also
have to have the mental state

00:19:50.040 --> 00:19:52.380
that goes along with that particular act.

00:19:52.380 --> 00:19:54.770
In this particular case,
President Trump needs to

00:19:54.770 --> 00:19:57.790
have had the specific
intent to solicit a bribe.

00:19:57.790 --> 00:19:59.610
Proving mens rea sounds hard.

00:19:59.610 --> 00:20:03.840
It requires proving the mental
state of another person,

00:20:03.840 --> 00:20:06.230
and oftentimes that is very difficult,

00:20:06.230 --> 00:20:08.820
but it's also something that
the criminal justice system

00:20:08.820 --> 00:20:10.705
is very, very familiar with.

00:20:10.705 --> 00:20:14.410
You use the witness's other
actions and statements

00:20:14.410 --> 00:20:16.550
to show a state of mind.

00:20:16.550 --> 00:20:18.430
Here for the most part, I
think we're talking about

00:20:18.430 --> 00:20:23.330
18 USC 201 b2, which is the
solicitation of a bribe.

00:20:23.330 --> 00:20:25.770
Now the caveat here is, as always,

00:20:25.770 --> 00:20:27.800
impeachment does not require proving

00:20:27.800 --> 00:20:30.770
beyond a reasonable doubt that
someone committed a crime.

00:20:30.770 --> 00:20:32.770
The burden for impeachment is not the same

00:20:32.770 --> 00:20:35.988
as the burden for a criminal prosecution.

00:20:35.988 --> 00:20:38.460
Impeachment is a political device

00:20:38.460 --> 00:20:40.780
and high crimes and
misdemeanors can often mean

00:20:40.780 --> 00:20:42.640
whatever Congress says it means.

00:20:42.640 --> 00:20:45.100
This particular statute does
provide a good definition

00:20:45.100 --> 00:20:47.060
of generally what courts look for

00:20:47.060 --> 00:20:48.870
in terms of solicitation of a bribe.

00:20:48.870 --> 00:20:51.810
Now the jury instructions
for federal bribery state

00:20:51.810 --> 00:20:53.430
that at least when you're talking about

00:20:53.430 --> 00:20:55.180
trying to bribe an official,

00:20:55.180 --> 00:20:57.470
the defendant must have
promised, offered, or given

00:20:57.470 --> 00:21:00.280
money or a thing of value
to the public official

00:21:00.280 --> 00:21:02.300
with the deliberate purpose of influencing

00:21:02.300 --> 00:21:04.277
an official act of that person.

00:21:04.277 --> 00:21:06.150
The analogy being that
when you're talking about

00:21:06.150 --> 00:21:08.500
the solicitation of a bribe,
it's the other way around,

00:21:08.500 --> 00:21:11.450
the public official is
asking to be influenced

00:21:11.450 --> 00:21:13.790
in exchange for some other official act,

00:21:13.790 --> 00:21:16.550
but that is the general mens rea,

00:21:16.550 --> 00:21:20.170
the intent requirement that a
prosecutor would have to show

00:21:20.170 --> 00:21:22.370
in order to prove solicitation of a bribe.

00:21:22.370 --> 00:21:25.010
Now here, on November 20th,
Gordon Sondland said that

00:21:25.010 --> 00:21:27.660
the president conditioned
a White House meeting

00:21:27.660 --> 00:21:30.280
on the Ukrainians
providing the investigation

00:21:30.280 --> 00:21:34.070
into the Bidens and the
Ukrainian Crowd Strike server,

00:21:34.070 --> 00:21:36.210
but Sondland also testified
that he never heard

00:21:36.210 --> 00:21:38.640
the specific words that the $400 million

00:21:38.640 --> 00:21:41.640
in military aid was conditioned
on the Biden investigation.

00:21:41.640 --> 00:21:43.610
He said that was his conclusion

00:21:43.610 --> 00:21:45.050
from all the instructions that he received

00:21:45.050 --> 00:21:47.130
from Mick Mulvaney and Mike Pompeo.

00:21:47.130 --> 00:21:48.520
Now contrary to what you see on TV,

00:21:48.520 --> 00:21:51.700
criminals rarely say the
actual explicit words

00:21:51.700 --> 00:21:55.740
that by themselves are sufficient
to prove the actual crime.

00:21:55.740 --> 00:21:57.780
Generally, prosecutors have to prove that

00:21:57.780 --> 00:21:59.290
with circumstantial evidence.

00:21:59.290 --> 00:22:01.460
Now also contrary to what you see on TV,

00:22:01.460 --> 00:22:03.955
circumstantial evidence
can be very, very strong.

00:22:03.955 --> 00:22:06.680
DNA evidence is considered
circumstantial evidence,

00:22:06.680 --> 00:22:08.970
and in fact if this were
a criminal prosecution,

00:22:08.970 --> 00:22:10.540
the jury would get an
instruction from the judge

00:22:10.540 --> 00:22:12.540
that says circumstantial
evidence is as strong,

00:22:12.540 --> 00:22:14.953
if not stronger as direct evidence.

00:22:14.953 --> 00:22:17.130
All that circumstantial evidence means is

00:22:17.130 --> 00:22:21.010
something that is not by
itself directly sufficient

00:22:21.010 --> 00:22:22.710
to prove the crime itself,

00:22:22.710 --> 00:22:24.220
or in this case, the mental state.

00:22:24.220 --> 00:22:27.240
Now in this particular case,
the circumstantial evidence

00:22:27.240 --> 00:22:30.360
of the potential mens rea
for solicitation of bribery

00:22:30.360 --> 00:22:33.940
would include the July 25th
call with President Zelensky,

00:22:33.940 --> 00:22:36.602
the July 26th call with Gordon Sondland,

00:22:36.602 --> 00:22:40.080
the fact that Rudolph
Giuliani was a go-between

00:22:40.080 --> 00:22:41.340
even though he's not a
member of the government,

00:22:41.340 --> 00:22:42.600
and everything else that was said and done

00:22:42.600 --> 00:22:46.160
as between Sondland and the
members of the State Department.

00:22:46.160 --> 00:22:48.150
In fact, Gordon Sondland testified that

00:22:48.150 --> 00:22:51.140
he cleared everything with
Mulvaney, Bolton, and Pompeo,

00:22:51.140 --> 00:22:54.350
and he assumed that if it
came from those people,

00:22:54.350 --> 00:22:57.730
that it came from orders
from the president himself.

00:22:57.730 --> 00:22:59.510
On the other hands, defenders
of the administration

00:22:59.510 --> 00:23:01.280
would point to other
circumstantial evidence

00:23:01.280 --> 00:23:03.450
that they would claims as evidence of

00:23:03.450 --> 00:23:05.733
lacking the required mens rea

00:23:05.733 --> 00:23:09.030
to effectuate a solicitation of bribery,

00:23:09.030 --> 00:23:11.550
and in fact, Gordon Sondland
says that after Bill Taylor

00:23:11.550 --> 00:23:13.677
famously texted "as I said on the phone,

00:23:13.677 --> 00:23:15.727
"I think it's crazy to
withhold security assistance

00:23:15.727 --> 00:23:17.900
"for help with a political campaign"

00:23:17.900 --> 00:23:20.740
in a September 9th phone
call with the president

00:23:20.740 --> 00:23:22.790
that potentially raises the questions

00:23:22.790 --> 00:23:23.930
that Bill Taylor Raised.

00:23:23.930 --> 00:23:26.150
- I just asked him an open-ended question,

00:23:26.150 --> 00:23:28.070
Mr. Chairman, what do
you want from Ukraine?

00:23:28.070 --> 00:23:31.220
I keep hearing all these
different ideas and theories

00:23:31.220 --> 00:23:33.820
and this and that, what do you want?

00:23:33.820 --> 00:23:37.340
And it was a very short,
abrupt conversation.

00:23:37.340 --> 00:23:39.563
He was not in a good mood,

00:23:39.563 --> 00:23:41.977
and he just said "I want
nothing, I want nothing,

00:23:41.977 --> 00:23:43.350
"I want no quid pro quo."

00:23:43.350 --> 00:23:45.370
- While you can argue that the president

00:23:45.370 --> 00:23:48.930
explicitly saying that he
doesn't want a quid pro quo

00:23:48.930 --> 00:23:51.090
is exculpatory evidence and shows that

00:23:51.090 --> 00:23:53.200
he lacks the requisite mens rea,

00:23:53.200 --> 00:23:54.810
you could also argue given the timeline

00:23:54.810 --> 00:23:57.240
that this actually supports
a potential coverup

00:23:57.240 --> 00:23:59.550
from the president,
that given the timeline

00:23:59.550 --> 00:24:01.930
that the Politico article
had already revealed

00:24:01.930 --> 00:24:04.510
the potentially improper hold

00:24:04.510 --> 00:24:06.160
on Ukrainian foreign assistance

00:24:06.160 --> 00:24:08.310
and the fact that even
Bill Taylor was saying

00:24:08.310 --> 00:24:11.137
it was crazy to condition the aid on

00:24:11.137 --> 00:24:13.860
the Ukrainians investigating the Bidens,

00:24:13.860 --> 00:24:16.884
that this call and the statement that

00:24:16.884 --> 00:24:19.370
President Trump made to Gordon Sondland

00:24:19.370 --> 00:24:21.540
is actually evidence
that he was backtracking

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:24.370
and trying to cover up his
tracks, it can go both ways.

00:24:24.370 --> 00:24:26.490
That's the issue with
circumstantial evidence,

00:24:26.490 --> 00:24:28.150
it can support one narrative,

00:24:28.150 --> 00:24:30.540
but it can also support a
different narrative as well,

00:24:30.540 --> 00:24:32.790
and I will leave it to you
as to whether you believe

00:24:32.790 --> 00:24:35.130
the president intended the
exchange for his own purpose

00:24:35.130 --> 00:24:37.590
or for an official
purpose of the government.

00:24:37.590 --> 00:24:40.070
Which brings me to the
next potential defense,

00:24:40.070 --> 00:24:42.680
which is that the president
controls foreign policy

00:24:42.680 --> 00:24:44.340
and it would be improper to impeach him

00:24:44.340 --> 00:24:46.430
over a foreign policy decision.

00:24:46.430 --> 00:24:48.580
Now there is certainly some truth to this.

00:24:48.580 --> 00:24:51.320
The president has almost all of the power

00:24:51.320 --> 00:24:53.760
for foreign policy, he
is the commander in chief

00:24:53.760 --> 00:24:56.680
of the armed forces and he
controls almost every decision

00:24:56.680 --> 00:24:58.420
when it comes to foreign policy.

00:24:58.420 --> 00:25:01.670
Really, Congress has the
power to declare war,

00:25:01.670 --> 00:25:04.200
ratify treaties, and appropriate funds

00:25:04.200 --> 00:25:07.490
when it comes to foreign policy,
but that's really about it.

00:25:07.490 --> 00:25:09.810
The executive is really in
control of foreign policy.

00:25:09.810 --> 00:25:12.070
Now there is a dispute about whether

00:25:12.070 --> 00:25:15.550
a constitutionally-enumerated
power can give rise

00:25:15.550 --> 00:25:18.363
to a crime or to impeachment.

00:25:18.363 --> 00:25:19.769
- Well when the president does it,

00:25:19.769 --> 00:25:22.010
that means that it is not illegal.

00:25:22.010 --> 00:25:23.280
- By definition?

00:25:23.280 --> 00:25:24.113
- Exactly.

00:25:24.113 --> 00:25:27.030
- But most constitutional
scholars agree that

00:25:27.030 --> 00:25:30.370
even an official act that is
enumerated by the Constitution

00:25:30.370 --> 00:25:34.370
can give rise to impeachment
if not criminal prosecution.

00:25:34.370 --> 00:25:36.730
That being said, you would imagine that

00:25:36.730 --> 00:25:40.284
given that the president has
vast foreign policy powers,

00:25:40.284 --> 00:25:43.040
that you would be very, very reluctant

00:25:43.040 --> 00:25:45.390
to impeach a president over something

00:25:45.390 --> 00:25:48.310
that he or she has plenary authority over,

00:25:48.310 --> 00:25:50.280
in this case, setting foreign policy

00:25:50.280 --> 00:25:52.430
or removing an ambassador.

00:25:52.430 --> 00:25:54.470
And there's no doubt that
interactions with Ukraine

00:25:54.470 --> 00:25:56.810
touch on foreign policy power,

00:25:56.810 --> 00:25:59.010
but just because you have
a right doesn't mean that

00:25:59.010 --> 00:26:02.490
you can escape the repercussions
of exercising that right.

00:26:02.490 --> 00:26:04.860
In the same way that you
have a First Amendment right

00:26:04.860 --> 00:26:07.560
to freedom of speech, but
that doesn't absolve you

00:26:07.560 --> 00:26:10.600
from all of the repercussions
of actually using

00:26:10.600 --> 00:26:12.120
that speech in a specific way.

00:26:12.120 --> 00:26:15.170
By analogy, consider a
hypothetical involving pardons.

00:26:15.170 --> 00:26:16.990
Now in the same way that the president has

00:26:16.990 --> 00:26:19.400
almost unfettered foreign policy power,

00:26:19.400 --> 00:26:22.230
the president absolutely
has unfettered pardon power.

00:26:22.230 --> 00:26:23.410
It can't be checked by Congress

00:26:23.410 --> 00:26:25.610
and it can't really be checked
by the judiciary either,

00:26:25.610 --> 00:26:28.410
but if a president started selling pardons

00:26:28.410 --> 00:26:30.350
for a million dollars apiece,

00:26:30.350 --> 00:26:34.120
that president could and pretty
much by all accounts should

00:26:34.120 --> 00:26:36.300
be impeached for that kind of action,

00:26:36.300 --> 00:26:38.300
despite the fact that
the president is allowed

00:26:38.300 --> 00:26:40.890
to pardon people under
almost any circumstances,

00:26:40.890 --> 00:26:43.560
and in fact, one might
argue that that president

00:26:43.560 --> 00:26:47.090
should be criminally prosecuted
for solicitation of a bribe.

00:26:47.090 --> 00:26:48.940
So the fact that this particular instance

00:26:48.940 --> 00:26:52.110
implicates foreign policy
should give everyone pause

00:26:52.110 --> 00:26:54.410
because the president
has wide powers here,

00:26:54.410 --> 00:26:56.762
but just because the
president has wide power

00:26:56.762 --> 00:26:59.040
doesn't act to absolve the president

00:26:59.040 --> 00:27:01.440
of potentially untoward activity.

00:27:01.440 --> 00:27:03.060
Which brings me to the next defense,

00:27:03.060 --> 00:27:04.800
which is that the State Department

00:27:04.800 --> 00:27:07.060
or Gordon Sondland went rogue.

00:27:07.060 --> 00:27:08.360
I think we're gonna be seeing more

00:27:08.360 --> 00:27:09.810
of this particular defense in the wake of

00:27:09.810 --> 00:27:12.320
Gordon Sondland's bombshell testimony,

00:27:12.320 --> 00:27:14.670
but at base, Gordon
Sondland testified that

00:27:14.670 --> 00:27:16.960
in a few calls he had with the president,

00:27:16.960 --> 00:27:19.350
he didn't explicitly
link releasing the aid

00:27:19.350 --> 00:27:21.560
with investigations into the Bidens,

00:27:21.560 --> 00:27:22.970
but that everyone knew that

00:27:22.970 --> 00:27:25.080
that was what the president wanted.

00:27:25.080 --> 00:27:27.090
That was the understanding amongst Pompeo,

00:27:27.090 --> 00:27:30.448
Volker, and Sondland,
AKA The Three Amigos.

00:27:30.448 --> 00:27:33.700
So potentially, those
three people could be

00:27:33.700 --> 00:27:35.670
the fall guys for the administration,

00:27:35.670 --> 00:27:37.830
saying that it wasn't the
president that ordered

00:27:37.830 --> 00:27:41.600
the conditionality of the
Biden investigation on the aid,

00:27:41.600 --> 00:27:44.800
but rather these State Department
officials that went rogue.

00:27:44.800 --> 00:27:47.230
The argument being that the
president didn't order it,

00:27:47.230 --> 00:27:50.480
if the State Department
officials had that understanding,

00:27:50.480 --> 00:27:52.850
it wasn't an understanding
that came from the president,

00:27:52.850 --> 00:27:55.520
and that the State Department
and the Chief of Staff

00:27:55.520 --> 00:27:57.690
slash the head of the OMB Mick Mulvaney

00:27:57.690 --> 00:28:01.480
effectively went rogue
in a coordinated effort

00:28:01.480 --> 00:28:03.190
to extract something from the Ukrainians

00:28:03.190 --> 00:28:05.060
that the president actually didn't want.

00:28:05.060 --> 00:28:06.860
I will leave it to you if you think that

00:28:06.860 --> 00:28:09.010
it is more likely that these individuals

00:28:09.010 --> 00:28:12.380
acted without the knowledge
and consent of the president,

00:28:12.380 --> 00:28:14.800
or whether it was more
likely that the president

00:28:14.800 --> 00:28:17.138
gave orders that were trickled through

00:28:17.138 --> 00:28:19.740
the Secretary of State and Chief of Staff.

00:28:19.740 --> 00:28:24.359
- Secretary Perry,
Ambassador Volker, and I

00:28:24.359 --> 00:28:29.210
worked with Mr. Rudy
Giuliani on Ukraine matters

00:28:29.210 --> 00:28:31.210
at the express direction

00:28:31.210 --> 00:28:33.200
of the president of the United States.

00:28:33.200 --> 00:28:36.280
- Now Gordon Sondland says
that when Giuliani gave orders,

00:28:36.280 --> 00:28:37.940
it was assumed to have come from

00:28:37.940 --> 00:28:39.420
the president, specifically.

00:28:39.420 --> 00:28:42.010
- When the president says
"talk to my personal attorney"

00:28:42.010 --> 00:28:44.996
and then Mr. Giuliani
as his personal attorney

00:28:44.996 --> 00:28:47.920
makes certain requests or demands,

00:28:47.920 --> 00:28:49.860
we assume it's coming from the president.

00:28:49.860 --> 00:28:52.230
- Now one issue with
this particular defense

00:28:52.230 --> 00:28:55.090
is that Rudy Giuliani
didn't have the authority

00:28:55.090 --> 00:28:58.550
to hold up Ukrainian aid
for almost an entire year,

00:28:58.550 --> 00:29:01.740
that that falls under
the purview of the OMB

00:29:01.740 --> 00:29:03.370
that's led by Mick Mulvaney,

00:29:03.370 --> 00:29:05.769
which is one reason we
would really want to know

00:29:05.769 --> 00:29:08.530
what people told Mick Mulvaney and why,

00:29:08.530 --> 00:29:11.090
as well as what was told to Rudy Giuliani

00:29:11.090 --> 00:29:13.300
and what was spoken
between the two of them,

00:29:13.300 --> 00:29:15.250
which brings me to the
next potential defense,

00:29:15.250 --> 00:29:17.790
the president has a duty
to root out corruption,

00:29:17.790 --> 00:29:19.710
both domestically and abroad.

00:29:19.710 --> 00:29:22.350
I think that this is
probably the main defense

00:29:22.350 --> 00:29:24.730
that we're going to see going forward.

00:29:24.730 --> 00:29:27.730
Now as we've covered, the
president controls foreign policy

00:29:27.730 --> 00:29:30.080
and there's no doubt that
rooting out corruption

00:29:30.080 --> 00:29:32.240
is intertwined with that mandate

00:29:32.240 --> 00:29:34.870
to deal with America's foreign policy.

00:29:34.870 --> 00:29:37.180
Now based on the evidence
that's been elicited so far,

00:29:37.180 --> 00:29:38.680
I leave it to you as
to whether you believe

00:29:38.680 --> 00:29:41.180
that the president was
motivated to root out

00:29:41.180 --> 00:29:43.900
corruption abroad, or
whether he was motivated

00:29:43.900 --> 00:29:45.860
to get dirt on a political rival,

00:29:45.860 --> 00:29:49.300
but note that people are
complicated and it can be both.

00:29:49.300 --> 00:29:50.850
People can be motivated by multiple

00:29:50.850 --> 00:29:52.790
different things at the same time.

00:29:52.790 --> 00:29:56.120
Now in the criminal world,
in terms of mens rea,

00:29:56.120 --> 00:29:58.860
if there are multiple reasons
for committing something,

00:29:58.860 --> 00:30:01.860
if any one of those mental
states is sufficient

00:30:01.860 --> 00:30:05.580
to meet the standard of
mens rea as required,

00:30:05.580 --> 00:30:09.130
that person can be convicted
of that particular crime.

00:30:09.130 --> 00:30:11.840
So the underlying argument
is that if there was a basis

00:30:11.840 --> 00:30:15.166
for the Hunter Biden
slash corruption argument,

00:30:15.166 --> 00:30:17.500
then the president is absolved,

00:30:17.500 --> 00:30:19.750
but that actually goes the wrong way,

00:30:19.750 --> 00:30:22.100
at least when you're talking
about a criminal context.

00:30:22.100 --> 00:30:24.730
If someone has committed
the act that is sufficient

00:30:24.730 --> 00:30:26.642
for a crime, say bank robbery,

00:30:26.642 --> 00:30:29.590
and has multiple reasons for doing it,

00:30:29.590 --> 00:30:32.600
one of which is the mental
state that's required

00:30:32.600 --> 00:30:36.170
for the criminal offense,
say bank robbery again,

00:30:36.170 --> 00:30:37.380
the fact that there are multiple

00:30:37.380 --> 00:30:39.320
different motivations is irrelevant.

00:30:39.320 --> 00:30:42.787
If any one part of that
motivation is sufficient,

00:30:42.787 --> 00:30:45.050
that person can be convicted.

00:30:45.050 --> 00:30:46.880
And again, we would have to go back

00:30:46.880 --> 00:30:48.730
to not only what the president has said,

00:30:48.730 --> 00:30:49.870
but what the president has done

00:30:49.870 --> 00:30:52.850
and what everyone else had
done around the president

00:30:52.850 --> 00:30:55.150
to determine what the
president's mental state

00:30:55.150 --> 00:30:58.420
actually was at the time and
whether that was sufficient

00:30:58.420 --> 00:31:00.810
to prove solicitation of bribery.

00:31:00.810 --> 00:31:03.310
Now Kurt Volker testified on November 19th

00:31:03.310 --> 00:31:05.440
that he sought investigations into Burisma

00:31:05.440 --> 00:31:07.770
to be separate from the Bidens.

00:31:07.770 --> 00:31:10.956
The former being okay,
the later being improper

00:31:10.956 --> 00:31:13.130
quid pro quo solicitation of bribery.

00:31:13.130 --> 00:31:16.120
- There was no mention
of Vice President Biden.

00:31:16.120 --> 00:31:19.880
Rather in referencing Burisma
in 2016 election interference,

00:31:19.880 --> 00:31:22.268
it was clear to me that he, Mr. Yarmouk,

00:31:22.268 --> 00:31:25.190
was only talking about
whether any Ukrainians

00:31:25.190 --> 00:31:26.950
had acted inappropriately.

00:31:26.950 --> 00:31:29.240
- He concluded that others
in the Trump administration

00:31:29.240 --> 00:31:32.647
saw the two as intertwined
and as the same.

00:31:32.647 --> 00:31:36.770
If that is indeed the case,
that could be sufficient

00:31:36.770 --> 00:31:39.044
to prove solicitation of bribery,

00:31:39.044 --> 00:31:41.470
but what could be
potentially even more damning

00:31:41.470 --> 00:31:45.706
in this context and might
obviate the corruption defense

00:31:45.706 --> 00:31:47.573
is that multiple witnesses had testified

00:31:47.573 --> 00:31:49.760
that President Trump wasn't interested

00:31:49.760 --> 00:31:51.450
in the investigation into the Bidens

00:31:51.450 --> 00:31:55.460
so much as the announcement
of the investigation.

00:31:55.460 --> 00:31:57.960
As I think Asha Rangappa
was the first to point out,

00:31:57.960 --> 00:32:00.020
this is what's considered
black propaganda,

00:32:00.020 --> 00:32:03.070
propaganda that obfuscates
where it came from.

00:32:03.070 --> 00:32:05.130
The Trump administration
wanted the Ukrainians

00:32:05.130 --> 00:32:07.110
to make an announcement
of the investigation

00:32:07.110 --> 00:32:09.230
into the Bidens and make it look like

00:32:09.230 --> 00:32:12.240
it had nothing to do with
the Trump administration.

00:32:12.240 --> 00:32:14.140
And on top of that, it doesn't appear that

00:32:14.140 --> 00:32:17.160
the Trump administration
cared about this corruption

00:32:17.160 --> 00:32:20.730
in 2017 or 2018 when hundreds
of millions of dollars

00:32:20.730 --> 00:32:23.700
of aid went to the Ukrainians,
it was only in 2019

00:32:23.700 --> 00:32:26.710
when Joe Biden became the
political front runner

00:32:26.710 --> 00:32:29.140
for the Democrats for the 2020 election.

00:32:29.140 --> 00:32:32.760
And since President Trump asks
us to read the transcript,

00:32:32.760 --> 00:32:35.600
in the transcript of the July 25th call,

00:32:35.600 --> 00:32:37.670
the president doesn't
actually mention corruption.

00:32:37.670 --> 00:32:39.910
He mentions the Bidens three times,

00:32:39.910 --> 00:32:42.770
and similarly, President Trump
doesn't ask about Burisma,

00:32:42.770 --> 00:32:44.090
but about the Bidens.

00:32:44.090 --> 00:32:46.960
Now other witnesses may have
a lot more to talk about this,

00:32:46.960 --> 00:32:49.500
that we may learn evidence
that the president

00:32:49.500 --> 00:32:51.860
was more concerned about corruption.

00:32:51.860 --> 00:32:54.354
Of course, as we've talked
about on this channel before,

00:32:54.354 --> 00:32:57.730
there are proper channels for
opening up an investigation

00:32:57.730 --> 00:32:59.780
into an American citizen abroad,

00:32:59.780 --> 00:33:02.650
and President Trump does
not appear to have followed

00:33:02.650 --> 00:33:04.510
any of those procedures, and of course,

00:33:04.510 --> 00:33:07.500
those procedures never involve
using your own personal

00:33:07.500 --> 00:33:11.080
attorney as a figurehead
for American foreign policy.

00:33:11.080 --> 00:33:13.440
And also, as several witnesses testified,

00:33:13.440 --> 00:33:16.440
foreign policy and/or the
National Security Council

00:33:16.440 --> 00:33:20.120
is supposed to be very separate
from domestic politics.

00:33:20.120 --> 00:33:22.180
So arguably it is correct that

00:33:22.180 --> 00:33:24.520
whatever Hunter Biden
may or may not have done,

00:33:24.520 --> 00:33:26.300
and whatever Joe Biden
may or may not have done

00:33:26.300 --> 00:33:28.730
isn't relevant to the
question of whether president

00:33:28.730 --> 00:33:30.910
engaged in some illicit action,

00:33:30.910 --> 00:33:34.160
but what is relevant is the
president's understanding

00:33:34.160 --> 00:33:37.550
of what Hunter and Joe Biden
may or may not have done.

00:33:37.550 --> 00:33:39.100
And I think this is what Ben Shapiro

00:33:39.100 --> 00:33:40.450
was getting at in his tweet.

00:33:40.450 --> 00:33:43.250
He accurately points out that
the president's motivations

00:33:43.250 --> 00:33:44.939
do matter in this context,

00:33:44.939 --> 00:33:49.376
that similar action can
be liable or culpable

00:33:49.376 --> 00:33:51.860
depending on the mental
state that's at issue,

00:33:51.860 --> 00:33:54.180
but at the same time,
the process matters too.

00:33:54.180 --> 00:33:57.900
Given how unusual some
of these actions are

00:33:57.900 --> 00:33:59.590
and how bad some of them look,

00:33:59.590 --> 00:34:02.450
it can be very, very
difficult to make a defense

00:34:02.450 --> 00:34:05.600
that the mental state is
missing in this particular case.

00:34:05.600 --> 00:34:08.290
The process does matter,
and you can't just put

00:34:08.290 --> 00:34:10.170
lipstick on a pig by saying that

00:34:10.170 --> 00:34:13.607
something that was completely
illegal and improper

00:34:13.607 --> 00:34:17.170
was done for the purpose
of rooting out corruption.

00:34:17.170 --> 00:34:20.030
For example, as law professor Orin Kerr

00:34:20.030 --> 00:34:23.627
said very facetiously, "you
can't impeach Nixon for trying

00:34:23.627 --> 00:34:26.937
"to uncover corruption at
the DNC's Watergate offices.

00:34:26.937 --> 00:34:28.867
"It was Nixon's duty to fight corruption

00:34:28.867 --> 00:34:30.727
"and it's not his fault
that his political opponents

00:34:30.727 --> 00:34:32.667
"were so corrupt that it required him

00:34:32.667 --> 00:34:34.467
"to send burglars over to break in.

00:34:34.467 --> 00:34:36.787
"Some might counter that a
president has better ways

00:34:36.787 --> 00:34:39.487
"to fight corruption,
such as sending the FBI,

00:34:39.487 --> 00:34:41.107
"whose job it is to do that.

00:34:41.107 --> 00:34:43.679
"But Nixon is so passionate
about fighting corruption

00:34:43.679 --> 00:34:45.907
"that he felt compelled to secretly send

00:34:45.907 --> 00:34:48.177
"his own burglars, loyal only to him,

00:34:48.177 --> 00:34:49.627
"to get it done right.

00:34:49.627 --> 00:34:52.797
"It's disgusting that some
think Nixon should be impeached

00:34:52.797 --> 00:34:54.797
"simply for loving America so much that

00:34:54.797 --> 00:34:56.857
"he just wanted to investigate corruption

00:34:56.857 --> 00:34:58.862
"in the most effective way he could.

00:34:58.862 --> 00:35:01.177
"You'd have to love
corruption to criticize

00:35:01.177 --> 00:35:02.367
"the Watergate break-in.

00:35:02.367 --> 00:35:04.440
"It was a perfect break-in."

00:35:04.440 --> 00:35:05.700
That really gets to the heart of it.

00:35:05.700 --> 00:35:07.400
The process matters,

00:35:07.400 --> 00:35:10.240
and the process also gives us a window

00:35:10.240 --> 00:35:13.430
into the motivations
that might have motivated

00:35:13.430 --> 00:35:16.250
the particular actions at issue here.

00:35:16.250 --> 00:35:18.490
Now I think I'll have to do
an entirely separate video

00:35:18.490 --> 00:35:21.450
on Hunter and Joe Biden and
the potential for corruption,

00:35:21.450 --> 00:35:23.930
but suffice to say both sides could be

00:35:23.930 --> 00:35:25.350
right at the same time.

00:35:25.350 --> 00:35:27.030
There could be underlying corruption

00:35:27.030 --> 00:35:29.030
and it could also be the
case that President Trump

00:35:29.030 --> 00:35:31.300
did something illegal and impeachable.

00:35:31.300 --> 00:35:33.530
Both could be true at the same time.

00:35:33.530 --> 00:35:35.857
Hunter Biden could be the devil incarnate,

00:35:35.857 --> 00:35:39.030
but President Trump's acts could still be

00:35:39.030 --> 00:35:41.600
an illegal solicitation of a bribe,

00:35:41.600 --> 00:35:43.720
they are not mutually exclusive,

00:35:43.720 --> 00:35:45.090
which is one of the reasons why I think

00:35:45.090 --> 00:35:48.210
a lot of president's defenders
will retreat to the position

00:35:48.210 --> 00:35:51.250
that it's bad, but it's
not impeachably bad.

00:35:51.250 --> 00:35:53.910
This is a political question
as to whether these actions

00:35:53.910 --> 00:35:57.110
give rise to impeachment
or whether it is prudent

00:35:57.110 --> 00:35:59.590
to conduct an impeachment
under the circumstances.

00:35:59.590 --> 00:36:01.800
It's a political question
that I will leave to you,

00:36:01.800 --> 00:36:03.630
but also remember that the standard is not

00:36:03.630 --> 00:36:05.380
whether this is a crime or not,

00:36:05.380 --> 00:36:07.370
it is whether it is an abuse of power

00:36:07.370 --> 00:36:09.430
that is sufficient for impeachment or not,

00:36:09.430 --> 00:36:11.250
and all I'll say is that
it might be a good time

00:36:11.250 --> 00:36:15.200
to read Federal 65 and 66
written by Alexander Hamilton,

00:36:15.200 --> 00:36:18.370
which gives a pretty good
summary of why the founders

00:36:18.370 --> 00:36:20.710
gave Congress the power to impeach

00:36:20.710 --> 00:36:22.830
and when it's a good idea to impeach.

00:36:22.830 --> 00:36:24.960
Always a good idea to read
The Federalist Papers.

00:36:24.960 --> 00:36:26.450
And then of course,
everything else pretty much

00:36:26.450 --> 00:36:28.620
falls into the Chewbacca defense.

00:36:28.620 --> 00:36:31.950
- Ladies and gentlemen, this is Chewbacca.

00:36:31.950 --> 00:36:33.220
Now think about that for one minute.

00:36:33.220 --> 00:36:35.100
That does not make sense.

00:36:35.100 --> 00:36:37.920
- Or in other words,
as lawyers like to say,

00:36:37.920 --> 00:36:40.220
when you don't have the
facts, pound the law,

00:36:40.220 --> 00:36:42.030
when you don't have the
law, pound the facts,

00:36:42.030 --> 00:36:43.830
and when you don't have
the law or the facts,

00:36:43.830 --> 00:36:47.510
pound the table.
(Chewbacca wailing)

00:36:47.510 --> 00:36:49.620
The funny thing is that
in my Twitter timeline,

00:36:49.620 --> 00:36:51.850
I am seeing both democrats and republicans

00:36:51.850 --> 00:36:54.860
using this phrase to
describe the other side,

00:36:54.860 --> 00:36:56.790
so I will leave it to you
as to whether you think

00:36:56.790 --> 00:36:59.050
that it is the republicans who are using

00:36:59.050 --> 00:37:00.027
the Chewbacca defense or the democrats

00:37:00.027 --> 00:37:01.800
who are using the Chewbacca defense.

00:37:01.800 --> 00:37:03.940
And of course, as Mark Twain said,

00:37:03.940 --> 00:37:06.720
history doesn't repeat, but it does rhyme.

00:37:06.720 --> 00:37:08.400
And I would say that
everything that is unfolding

00:37:08.400 --> 00:37:10.730
in this impeachment inquiry rhymes with

00:37:10.730 --> 00:37:12.150
the Nixon impeachment proceedings,

00:37:12.150 --> 00:37:14.520
and if you're not familiar with
those impeachment hearings,

00:37:14.520 --> 00:37:16.760
you are missing a huge part of the story.

